<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Monte Naranco</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Monte Naranco is a hill overlooking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. On its slopes King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramiro-i-of-asturias">Ramiro I of Asturias</a> built a royal complex in the 840s, of which two buildings survive: the palace hall later converted into the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santa-maria-del-naranco">Santa María del Naranco</a>, dedicated in 848, and the church of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-miguel-de-lillo">San Miguel de Lillo</a>. They are outstanding works of Asturian pre-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanesque-architecture">Romanesque architecture</a> and part of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/monte-naranco.jpg" alt="Monte Naranco" width="320" height="175" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vsuarezp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Monte_Naranco_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
